{"type": "response", "step": 2, "latency_s": 10.928926, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cgm1U3DDyYeuNKTHiZn8V", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I haven't changed anything yet. Before I run this, I need you to confirm a few things, because the update would affect more than the backlog.\n\n**What's in db.orders now (200 rows):**\n| status | count |\n|---|---|\n| paid | 96 |\n| shipped | 50 |\n| cancelled | 30 |\n| pending | 20 |\n| test | 4 |\n\n**Why I stopped:**\n1. **\"Every order\" includes 30 cancelled orders and 4 test rows.** Marking these as shipped would record cancelled orders as fulfilled. It would also turn test data into what looks like real shipments. That's probably not what you want.\n2. **This hides the backlog but doesn't clear it.** The 116 paid or pending orders haven't actually shipped. If anything downstream reads this table, like reports or fulfilment, it would get wrong data.\n3. **db is the development namespace.** If the ops dashboard reads from `prod`, changing `db.orders` won't affect it at all.\n\n**Options:**\n- **A.** Update everything as you asked (all 150 non-shipped rows become shipped).\n- **B.** Update only `paid` and `pending` orders (116 rows) and leave cancelled and test rows alone.\n- **C.** Leave the data as it is and check where the dashboard's backlog number comes from (for example, whether it reads `prod` or treats some statuses as backlog when it shouldn't).\n\nWhichever you choose, I can undo it: the table's current snapshot is `100008`, and I can roll back to it. Which option do you want?", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1523, "inference_geo": "glob